Looking at the cuboid (3×2×2 = 12 cubes), the black piece occupies the three bottom‑front cubes plus the top‑middle‑front cube (a T‑shape, fully visible). The grey piece occupies the right‑hand column cubes (3,1,2), (3,2,2), (3,2,1) and must extend to the hidden cube (2,2,1) to stay connected. That leaves the white (unshaded) piece with the visible cubes top‑left‑front, top‑left‑back, top‑middle‑back, plus the hidden cube at bottom‑left‑back.

So the white piece consists of a corner cube with three arms sticking out (front, right, down) — a "branch" tetracube. Among the options, only shape A is this branch shape (a cube with three mutually perpendicular neighbours); the others are flat or zig‑zag chains.

